what do an icy despot and a  crustacean-themed conqueror have in  common game of thrones and house of the  dragon viewers may notice some shared  traits spoilers for house of the dragon  ahead  the ending of the second episode of  house of the dragon introduces viewers  to cragus drajar also known as crab  feeder whose nickname tells you  everything you need to know about his  preferred method of execution and body  disposal a high ranking member of the  triarchy crab feeder has conquered the  strategically important step stones  islands as a result the era's finest  seafarer and reigning master of ships  lord corliss velarion isn't very happy  i will not have driftmark beggared while  our king idles himself with feasts and  balls and tawnies the sea snake forms an  alliance with disgraced damon targaryen  and in episode 3 they face off with crab  feeders forces on the stepstones in a  desperate bid to get the win before  reinforcements come in damon hatches a  daring plan to use himself as bait and  pretend to surrender to draw crab feeder  out from the caves he uses to avoid  dragonfire against all odds a desperate  maneuver works and after a thrilling  battle sequence daemon chases crab peter  down and kills him with his ravaged  features and golden face mask crab  beater is a very memorable looking guy  there's a time skip of three years in  the third episode of house of the dragon  and crab peter has been holding the  stepstones that whole time even so he  seems to go down easily when the time  comes grabbed reader's quick end has  some comparing him to the game of  thrones villain the night king and not  in a very good way they're both visually  interesting and super threatening  villains who mostly just stand around  looking creepy while everyone else does  the majority of the fighting and then  when the time comes for them to die  they're both dispatched fairly quickly  apart from the fact that crab peter  isn't a supernatural threat there's of  course one significant difference by the  time arya stark stabs the night king to  death in game of thrones the villain has  been a looming threat since season 4. as  such while the leader of the white  walkers does go down over the course of  a single episode at least he's been  around for a while  what does he want  an endless knight  he wants to erase this world  crab feeder on the other hand only gets  that ominous intro at the end of episode  two the show then fast forwards through  all of his cool moments of which there  must have been many considering that he  seems to have been successfully holding  the stepstones for several years  we don't even see his gruesome death  scene damon by sex crab feeder  off-screen  one would assume house of the dragon is  trying to avoid making the mistakes many  fans think game of thrones made in its  final season with author george rr  martin's increased involvement and the  benefit of hindsight it's easy to  imagine that the prequel will look to  steer clear of the events that led to  rampant criticism of the last few  episodes of game of thrones the night  king swift and for some anti-climactic  downfall after seasons of build-up seems  like the kind of thing house of the  dragon wouldn't be willing to touch with  a 10-foot pole by introducing crab  feeder in a big bad fashion and doing  away with the character after one  episode of hectic battle also the dragon  may deliberately be introducing similar  seeming storylines but tweaking them so  they have a different impact it's also  entirely possible that house of the  dragon is using the shorthand of a  similar storyline as a means to build up  daemon as a shrewd operator still it's  pretty strange that the show's writers  seemingly chose to call back to the  original show's least loved era unless  it's unintentional which could indicate  a whole different set of issues  [Music]
 

